  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 143 AM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nA weak wave of low pressure is slowly sliding westward along the \nnorth-central Gulf coastline around the periphery of mid-level high \npressure that is sliding more so into the Southeast US. For the \nshort term, our PWATs generally range from 1.5-1.8\", which falls \nbelow 2 standard deviations from our climatological normal. This \njust means that whatever seabreeze shower/storm that does develop \nwill be isolated in nature and short-lived. In fact, most of the \nglobal models want to keep the majority of the 2.0-2.3\" PWATs \noffshore and approaching the Southeast Texas/Louisiana border area \nby Tuesday (the ECMWF ensemble is more moist in PWATs moving \nonshore). That is when the Victoria Crossroads and locations north \nof Matagorda Bay will see 20-40% chances of rain. The real question \nis how far west this area of higher PWATs move into South Texas. If \nwe see closer to 2.0\" PWATs, the more efficient will our seabreeze \nactivity be. \n\nBy mid-week, whatever comes from the wave moves onshore, but the mid-\nlevel flow around the now elongated high pressure system will keep \nthis higher PWAT advection into the western Gulf. This will keep the \nshower activity offshore a possibility through the upcoming weekend, \nbut less so confidence as to how much this shower activity can make \nit onshore. For now 10-30% rain chances for the end of the forecast \nperiod will primarily be for the coastal zones and less so for the \nBrush Country. \n\nWhat will be ever present regardless of shower activity occurs will \nbe the HeatRisk, which is forecast to top at major/extreme (level 3 \nand 4 of 4) for inland locations. While us South Texans are aware \nhow hot it can still be in September, let's not be complacent in \nkeeping ourselves hydrated and recognizing the signs of heat-related \nillness.",
